A smart contract is a self-executing contract with the terms of the agreement directly written into code. The code and the agreements contained therein exist across a distributed, decentralized blockchain network.

Here is an example of a simple smart contract written in Solidity, a popular language for Ethereum smart contracts:
```
pragma solidity ^0.8.0;

contract SimpleContract {
    uint public storedData;

    function set(uint x) public {
        storedData = x;
    }

    function get() public view returns (uint) {
        return storedData;
    }
}
```
This simple smart contract has two functions, `set` and `get`. The `set` function allows users to store a number, and the `get` function allows users to retrieve the number that was stored.

Keep in mind that this is a very basic example of a smart contract, and most real-world smart contracts will be much more complex. Additionally, smart contracts can be written in other languages such as Vyper, and can be executed on different blockchain platforms such as EOSIO, Stellar, etc.

It is important to note that writing secure smart contract is a challenging task that requires a deep understanding of blockchain technology, programming and security best practices. It is recommended to use experienced developers, test thoroughly and use formal verification methods when possible.

